 forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Texas remains a Republican-leaning state at the presidential level, and the forecast starts from that fundamental lean of roughly six points. The incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party benefits from incumbency, and no general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are yet available for this race. Headlines show growing Democratic interest in competing in Texas, but that interest has not yet translated into measurable evidence.\n\nWith only fundamentals to go on, the race is rated Lean Republican. The state's recent presidential margins have consistently favored Republicans, while demographic change and growing urban turnout have narrowed but not closed the gap. Uncertainty is wide because the general-election matchup is undefined and no polling exists; a defined ticket, sustained Democratic investment, or a national environment shifting several points could move Texas closer to competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2466, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2898, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2886, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2879, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2903, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2829, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2913, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2909, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.2878, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2866, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.288, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.2839, "margin": -6.0}]}